{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T14:33:10.816290", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Threonine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct the threonine carbon skeleton by cyanide homologation of acetaldehyde to lactaldehyde, followed by Strecker chemistry or hydantoin-mediated amino-acid formation. The ten pathways are distinct operational graph paths, not ten independently validated prebiotic syntheses: they combine two photoredox donor systems, imine-first versus cyanohydrin-storage entry, and alternative hydrolysis regimes. Cyanohydrin-storage pathways return to the same imine manifold and do not represent a new carbon\u2013nitrogen bond-forming mechanism. Selective lactonitrile photoreduction and sufficiently rapid hydantoin deprotection are important conditional bottlenecks; no complete end-to-end prebiotic synthesis is claimed to have been demonstrated. All processes are anoxic, and no enzymes or molecular oxygen are assumed. The designated target is L-threonine, but achiral chemistry also produces D-threonine and allo-threonines; no enantiomeric enrichment or threo-selective isolation is asserted. Unspecified stereochemistry in precursor nodes denotes unresolved stereochemical pools. Product lists enumerate possible stereoisomers without specifying their proportions. Neutral parent formulas are used where practical, with aqueous protonation and spectator counterions implicit. Because the schema lacks a coproduct role, non-target terminal products are labeled intermediate. For species formed in multiple settings, environment_formed records the principal setting, while reaction nodes identify additional formation environments.\n\nMOLECULES (21 nodes):\n  - mol_001: C2H4O (Acetaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_002: HCN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_005: SO3^2- (Sulfite ion) [starting]\n  - mol_006: C3H5NO (Lactonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C3H6O2 (Lactaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: NH3 (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_009: S8 (Elemental sulfur) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: SO4^2- (Sulfate ion) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C3H7NO (Lactaldehyde imine) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C4H7NO2 (Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C4H8N2O (Threonine-family aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C4H10N2O2 (Threoninamide-family intermediate) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_015: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_016: C5H8N2O3 (Threonine-family hydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: C5H10N2O4 (N-Carbamoylthreonine-family intermediate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C4H9NO3 (L-Threonine) [target]\n  - mol_019: C4H9NO3 (D-Threonine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C4H9NO3 (L-Allothreonine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_021: C4H9NO3 (D-Allothreonine) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (13 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_006 (Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_006, mol_004, mol_003 -> mol_007, mol_008, mol_009 (Conditional sulfide-driven photoreductive homologation of lactonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_006, mol_005, mol_003 -> mol_007, mol_008, mol_010 (Conditional sulfite-ferrocyanide photoreductive homologation of lactonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_007, mol_008 -> mol_011, mol_003 (Lactaldehyde imine formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_011, mol_002 -> mol_013 (Cyanide capture of lactaldehyde imine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_007, mol_002 -> mol_012 (Lactaldehyde capture as a cyanohydrin reservoir) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_012, mol_008 -> mol_011, mol_002, mol_003 (Ammonia-driven transfer from cyanohydrin reservoir to imine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_013, mol_003 -> mol_014 (Aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide-family intermediates) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_014, mol_003 -> mol_018, mol_019, mol_020, mol_021, mol_008 (Acidic threoninamide hydrolysis) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_014, mol_003 -> mol_018, mol_019, mol_020, mol_021, mol_008 (Alkaline threoninamide hydrolysis) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_013, mol_015 -> mol_016 (Bucherer-Bergs capture of the aminonitrile as a hydantoin) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_016, mol_003 -> mol_017 (Hydantoin ring opening to N-carbamoyl amino acids) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_013: mol_017, mol_003 -> mol_018, mol_019, mol_020, mol_021, mol_008, mol_015 (Nonenzymatic N-carbamoyl removal) [Hydrothermal]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Sulfide photoredox, imine-first, acidic hydrolysis (6 steps)\n  - P2: Sulfide photoredox, imine-first, alkaline hydrolysis (6 steps)\n  - P3: Sulfite photoredox, imine-first, acidic hydrolysis (6 steps)\n  - P4: Sulfite photoredox, imine-first, alkaline hydrolysis (6 steps)\n  - P5: Sulfide photoredox, cyanohydrin reservoir, acidic hydrolysis (7 steps)\n  - P6: Sulfide photoredox, cyanohydrin reservoir, alkaline hydrolysis (7 steps)\n  - P7: Sulfite photoredox, cyanohydrin reservoir, acidic hydrolysis (7 steps)\n  - P8: Sulfite photoredox, cyanohydrin reservoir, alkaline hydrolysis (7 steps)\n  - P9: Sulfide photoredox followed by hydantoin capture and deprotection (7 steps)\n  - P10: Sulfite photoredox followed by hydantoin capture and deprotection (7 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no co