specific rate measurements, including aminonitrile decomposition and subsequent amide hydrolysis. An acidic surface compartment is specified rather than treating all surface water as equivalent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from volcanic chloride acidity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately 0.1-1 M acid, with days-to-weeks kinetic screening. Subsequent glycine aldol chemistry requires transport into a different, alkaline compartment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed addition-elimination at the amide carbonyl releases the carboxylic acid and ammonia, present predominantly as ammonium in this compartment.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is established, but environmental acid supply and residence time must be sufficient. Glycine is considerably less structurally vulnerable than the later unsaturated or beta-hydroxy intermediates; this does not establish the downstream aldol reaction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Alternative glycine supply by glyoxylate reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Exposed native Ni-Fe alloy surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 1-20 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native Ni-Fe alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: pH 6.5-8.5, ammonia in excess, millimolar glyoxylate, sulfide-poor rock-water interface. Product must cool before surface concentration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine/hemiaminal ensemble with glyoxylate; metal-activated hydrogen reduces the carbon-nitrogen unsaturation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reductive amination is established chemistry, and native Ni-Fe can activate hydrogen. This exact glyoxylate/alloy operating window is an unvalidated geochemical implementation, not a demonstrated yield claim. Direct carbonyl reduction, catalyst passivation, and limited native-alloy exposure compete. This pathway supplies glycine independently of cyanide but still shares the speculative aldol finish.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate carbonyl-assisted glycine-acetaldehyde aldol addition\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Glyoxylate as a proposed reversible imine-forming catalyst\",\n        \"Ligand-bound Fe2+ as a proposed Lewis-acid cocatalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar, restricted headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Proposed glyoxylate/Fe2+ carbonyl-catalysis system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Unvalidated screening window: pH 8.5-10, 10-100 mM glycine, 1-100 mM acetaldehyde, substoichiometric glyoxylate and Fe2+. Sulfide-poor shaded pool; Fe precipitation and aldehyde loss must be measured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A transient glycine-derived imine would undergo alpha-carbon deprotonation, addition to acetaldehyde, and imine hydrolysis, regenerating the carbonyl catalyst.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE and a major bottleneck. The carbon-carbon bond construction is constitutionally correct, but no evidence supplied here establishes that glyoxylate/Fe2+ sufficiently activates free glycine und