dehyde in excess over acetaldehyde; dilute-to-moderately concentrated shaded pool. Reaction mixture is transferred rather than assumed to contain pure product.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"An acetaldehyde enolate adds to formaldehyde, giving the beta-hydroxy aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the correct aldol connectivity: the product is 3-hydroxypropanal, not lactaldehyde. Acetaldehyde self-aldol chemistry and further condensation compete, so environmental mixtures and actual selectivity must be measured. No mineral is added without a necessary catalytic role.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"3-Hydroxypropanal dehydration to acrolein\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from volcanic chloride acidity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 2-4 window during partial drying; short heated residence followed by cooling. Acrolein volatilization and polymerization preclude assuming quantitative transfer.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-promoted elimination from the beta-hydroxy aldehyde gives the conjugated enal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Beta-hydroxy carbonyl dehydration is established chemistry. The stated sequence requires a real pH change from the preceding alkaline pool, plus acrolein retention. Hydration equilibrium and polymerization determine whether a usable acrolein flux exists.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate acrolein Strecker assembly\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar, restricted headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 7.5-9 window; low acrolein concentration with continuous or pulsed delivery into ammonia/cyanide solution. Avoid storing concentrated acrolein.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Desired carbonyl imination followed by cyanide 1,2-addition gives an aminonitrile while retaining the alkene.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE substrate-specific implementation. Strecker chemistry supplies the reaction-class precedent (Strecker, 1850), not evidence for selective reaction of acrolein. Conjugate cyanide/ammonia addition and oligomerization are major competitors. Demonstrating useful yield of the specified unsaturated aminonitrile is a separate bottleneck before considering alkene hydration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Unsaturated aminonitrile hydration to vinylglycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from volcanic chloride acidity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"335-360 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1-2 window; time-resolved screening must distinguish nitrile hydration, alkene reaction, and aminonitrile destruction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile hydration gives the amide while the desired branch retains the carbon-carbon double bond.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile hydration is established, but selective preservation of this unsaturated aminonitrile framework is unverified here. This edge denotes one possible reaction channel, not an assumption that the amide can be isolated cleanly under the stated conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Vinylglycinamide hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from volcanic chloride acidity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"345-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-1 M acid window; days-to-weeks screening. Analyze alkene retention, cyclization products, and amino-acid recovery concurrently.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amide hydrolysis releases vinylglycine and ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The hydrolysis transformation is conventional, but a productive substrate-specific window has not been demonstrated here. Alkene hydration and intramolecular trapping may compete during the same exposure, so this pathway cannot be interpreted as a sequence of automatically separable pure compounds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate Markovnikov hydration of vinylglycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from strongly concentrated volcanic chloride acidity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Unvalidated screening window: approximately 0.3-2 M acid, hours to weeks. Such concentrated anoxic acid pools are a required, unestablished local setting, not a generic surface condition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The desired channel protonates the terminal alkene and adds water at C3, forming the beta-hydroxy amino-acid skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE and a major bottleneck. Markovnikov hydration gives the correct connectivity, but useful hydration of protonated vinylglycine is not established by analogy alone. Intramolecular lactonization, alternative hydration products, rearrangement, and decomposition may compete or dominate. The threonine fraction and recoverable yield must be measured; this is not a validated replacement for the previous photoreduction bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate alkene hydration at the vinylglycinamide stage\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from strongly concentrated volcanic chloride acidity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"335-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Unvalidated screening window: approximately 0.3-2 M acid. Measure both alkene hydration and amide hydrolysis; neither transformation is assumed to be independently switchable.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Desired acid-catalyzed hydration places hydroxyl at C3 before the amide is hydrolyzed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE. This is a chemically different transformation order with a distinct intermediate, not merely a different environmental label. It may avoid carboxyl-assisted lactonization, but protonated amino-group effects and competing hydrolysis remain. No superiority over hydration after amide hydrolysis is established.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate alkene hydration at the aminonitrile stage\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from strongly concentrated volcanic chloride acidity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"325-355 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Unvalidated screening window: approximately 0.3-2 M acid with short initial exposures and kinetic sampling. Immediate competing nitrile hydration and retro-Strecker chemistry are expected possibilities.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Desired Markovnikov alkene hydration introduces the beta-hydroxyl group while retaining the nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE. The net transformation is balanced and constitutionally appropriate, but acid-sensitive aminonitrile survival is a particularly serious limitation. This route is retained as a testable reaction-order branch, not evidence that early hydration improves yield or bypasses the general hydration bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Metal-sulfide-associated carbonylation to methyl thioacetate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Freshly precipitated FeS/NiS phases\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-390 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Proposed 10-100 bar total with a CO-bearing fluid or gas phase\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS-associated nickel and iron sites\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 5.5-7.5, anoxic CO and methanethiol supply, freshly exposed sulfide surfaces. Product export must compete with thioester hydrolysis; environmental CO and thiol concentrations are unresolved.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-associated carbonylation and sulfur-acyl transfer produce the acetyl thioester. Neutral net bookkeeping is two methanethiol molecules plus carbon monoxide yielding methyl thioacetate and hydrogen sulfide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Activated acetate formation from CO and methanethiol in Fe/Ni-sulfide systems has experimental precedent (Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, 1997). That study does not validate the entire proposed pressure/concentration range or downstream thioester condensation. Fe/Ni sulfides are included for their specific carbonylation chemistry, not as generic prebiotic catalysts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate thioester Claisen condensation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_023\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ligand-bound Fe2+ as a proposed enolate-stabilizing Lewis acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"315-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hypothetical Fe2+-assisted thioester condensation\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Unvalidated screening window: concentrated, partially drying film, pH 9-11. Requires cooling and concentration of exported thioester without prior hydrolysis; no quantitative transport efficiency is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A proposed thioester enolate attacks a second thioester, followed by methanethiolate elimination, producing the beta-ketothioester.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE and the principal carbon-skeleton bottleneck of the acetoacetate family. Thioester Claisen condensation is established in activated synthetic and enzymatic settings, but useful nonenzymatic condensation of methyl thioacetate under these mild aqueous conditions is not demonstrated here. Weak alpha-carbon activation, Fe precipitation, and hydrolysis may prevent accumulation entirely. The mineral-mediated acetyl-thioester precedent must not be extrapolated into evidence for this step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Beta-ketothioester methanolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar, restricted gas exchange\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed methanol-enriched microphase, pH 6-8, limited water activity. Methanol delivery must exceed volatilization; competing hydrolysis is explicitly expected.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Methanol attacks the activated thioester carbonyl and replaces the methylthio group, forming the oxygen ester.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thioester alcoholysis is established acyl-transfer chemistry. The methyl ester is chemically useful because it postpones exposure of a readily decarboxylating beta-keto acid. However, adequate methanol enrichment and alcoholysis selectivity over hydrolysis are unresolved geochemical requirements, not assumed consequences of wet-dry cycling.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Activated-methylene nitrosation of methyl acetoacetate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_026\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium in a nitrite-bearing acidic compartment\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar, restricted gas exchange\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 2-4, pulsed nitrite/nitrous-acid supply, low dissolved sulfide and low reactive Fe2+. Keep separate from hydrogenation interfaces until nitrosating species are consumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The beta-ketoester enol undergoes C-nitrosation; nitroso-oxime tautomerization gives the alpha-oximino beta-ketoester.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrosation of activated methylene compounds to oximes is established chemistry and supplies nitrogen at the correct carbon without an enzyme. Nitrite is an oxidized feedstock but is not molecular oxygen. Its local accumulation on an anoxic, Fe2+-rich Earth is a substantial supply problem because reduced iron and sulfide consume it; the reaction therefore requires a separate, transient compartment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate ketone-first reduction of the oxime ester\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_028\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Exposed native Ni-Fe alloy surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-380 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 5-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native Ni-Fe alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Unvalidated screening window: pH 5.5-8, sulfide-poor interface, residual nitrite removed by prior reaction rather than assumed compatible with the metal. Monitor competing oxime reduction and ester hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-activated hydrogen reduces the ketone to a secondary alcohol while the selected reaction channel retains the oxime.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ketone hydrogenation is established, but selective ketone-first reduction of this oxime ester on native alloy is speculative. Oxime reduction can occur concurrently, and sulfur carryover can poison the catalyst. This edge describes a possible reduction order, not a demonstrated selectively controllable operation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_023\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxime reduction after ketone reduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_028\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Exposed native Ni-Fe alloy surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-385 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 5-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native Ni-Fe alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 5.5-8 screening window; sulfide-poor interface. Follow N-O cleavage, carbon-nitrogen reduction, ester survival, and amino-alcohol recovery independently.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the oxime and hydrogenolytic N-O cleavage give the amino group and water; two hydrogen molecules are required by the inferred net stoichiometry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxime-to-amine reduction is established synthetic chemistry, but native-mineral activity and selectivity for this substrate are not demonstrated here. Over-reduction, oxime hydrolysis, and adsorption losses remain possible. Product stereochemistry is not controlled by the proposed achiral catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_024\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate oxime-first reduction of the beta-ketoester\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_029\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Exposed native Ni-Fe alloy surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-375 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 5-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native Ni-Fe alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Unvalidated screening window: pH 5.5-7.5, short residence followed by continued hydrogenation. Avoid prolonged storage of the alpha-amino beta-ketoester.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxime hydrogenation and N-O cleavage introduce the amino group before the ketone is reduced.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The reaction class is established, but selective accumulation of this amino-ketoester on a native alloy is speculative. Competing ketone reduction and decomposition may prevent isolation. This is a distinct mechanistic order within the same hydrogenation manifold, not an independent source of confidence in the upstream carbon skeleton.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_025\",\n      \"name\": \"Ketone reduction after oxime reduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_029\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Exposed native Ni-Fe alloy surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-375 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 5-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native Ni-Fe alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 5.5-7.5; hydrogenation must compete successfully with amino-ketoester condensation, ester hydrolysis, and subsequent beta-ketoacid decarboxylation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the remaining ketone forms the beta-hydroxyl group of methyl threoninate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ketone reduction gives the correct amino-alcohol connectivity. A sufficiently rapid mineral-catalyzed reaction before substrate degradation is an unresolved operational requirement. The ester protects against immediate beta-ketoacid decarboxylation only while it remains intact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_026\",\n      \"name\": \"Methyl threoninate hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from volcanic chloride acidity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1-2, water-rich receiving pool after cooling from the hydrogenation zone; hours-to-days screening followed by dilution or cooling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed ester hydrolysis releases the amino acid and methanol.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ester hydrolysis is a conventional and generally less demanding deprotection than hydantoin cleavage. It avoids the previous hydantoin bottleneck, but substrate-specific recovery and the necessary acid compartment still require verification. The reaction does not establish which stereoisomers were formed upstream.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_027\",\n      \"name\": \"Alternative ester hydrolysis before reduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_031\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from volcanic chloride acidity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Unvalidated pH 1-2 screening window, followed by prompt transfer and pH adjustment into a hydrogenating interface. Measure decarboxylation throughout hydrolysis, not only after transfer.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis removes the methyl ester while retaining the oxime and ketone in the desired channel.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ester hydrolysis is established, and this branch represents a chemically possible consequence of rewetting rather than an additional activation step. However, the resulting oxime-containing beta-keto acid may decarboxylate before reduction. A recoverable flux through this branch is unproven; it is not asserted to be preferable to retaining the ester.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_028\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate reductive trapping of the free oxime beta-keto acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_031\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_032\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Exposed native Ni-Fe alloy surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-365 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 5-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native Ni-Fe alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Unvalidated screening window: pH 5-7, rapid flow into a sulfide-poor hydrogenating interface. Reduction must outcompete decarboxylation during transport and heating.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Desired ketone hydrogenation removes the beta-keto motif and forms the beta-hydroxy oxime acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE and a major additional bottleneck for P10. Ketone reduction could stabilize the carbon skeleton, but neither its rate relative to decarboxylation nor its selectivity over oxime reduction is established. This explicit competition prevents treating free-acid formation as automatically productive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_029\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxime-acid reduction to the amino acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_032\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Exposed native Ni-Fe alloy surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-375 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 5-50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Native Ni-Fe alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 5-7, sulfide-poor interface; cool and remove product from prolonged metal contact after reaction. Amino-acid recovery and stereoisomer distribution require measurement.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxime reduction and N-O bond cleavage generate the amino group, releasing water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The oxime-to-amine transformation is established at the reaction-class level, but this free hydroxy-oxime acid/native-alloy combination is unvalidated. This edge avoids a final protecting-group cleavage without resolving the earlier free-beta-ketoacid survival problem. It therefore cannot independently substantiate a complete prebiotic pathway.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker-derived glycine followed by direct aldol assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde supplies glycine through aminonitrile and amide intermediates; proposed carbonyl-assisted addition to acetaldehyde then constructs the target skeleton. The direct glycine aldol step is unvalidated and rate-limiting in the evidential sense.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive-amination-derived glycine followed by aldol assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate reductive amination replaces cyanide-dependent glycine supply. This is an upstream-source alternative, not an independent validation of the shared speculative glycine-acetaldehyde aldol finish.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon-skeleton assembly at the glycinamide stage\",\n      \"description\": \"Aminoacetonitrile is hydrated only to the amide before proposed acetaldehyde addition. Subsequent threoninamide hydrolysis completes the constitutional target. Glycinamide carbon activation and the later hydrolysis/survival window remain unverified.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbon-skeleton assembly at the aminoacetonitrile stage\",\n      \"description\": \"Proposed acetaldehyde addition precedes both nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis. This uses a distinct beta-hydroxy aminonitrile intermediate but requires demonstrated carbon-carbon coupling and aminonitrile survival.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Unsaturated Strecker route with amino-acid-stage hydration\",\n      \"description\": \"Cross-aldol/dehydration supplies acrolein. Desired Strecker chemistry and complete nitrile hydrolysis produce vinylglycine, whose proposed Markovnikov hydration gives the target connectivity. Acrolein selectivity and vinylglycine hydration are separate major bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Unsaturated Strecker route with amide-stage hydration\",\n      \"description\": \"The alkene is hydrated at the vinylglycinamide stage, followed by amide hydrolysis. This is a chemically distinct transformation order, but no evidence establishes selective control or improved yield relative to P5.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Unsaturated Strecker route with aminonitrile-stage hydration\",\n      \"description\": \"Alkene hydration is attempted before nitrile hydration, converging on the beta-hydroxy aminonitrile used in P4. Strong-acid exposure of the aminonitrile adds a substantial survival risk; this is not a validated shortcut.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Thioester-acetoacetate route with ketone-first reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Carbonylation supplies an acetyl thioester; a speculative Claisen condensation builds the C4 skeleton. Methanolysis and nitrosation give an oxime beta-ketoester, followed by ketone reduction, oxime reduction, and ester hydrolysis. The thioester condensation and mineral reduction selectivity are unvalidated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_021\", \"rxn_022\", \"rxn_023\", \"rxn_026\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_025\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_028\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Thioester-acetoacetate route with oxime-first reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"The same oxime beta-ketoester is reduced in the opposite chemical order, passing through an amino-ketoester before methyl threoninate. This is a mechanistic-order alternative, not independent evidence for carbon-skeleton formation or a selectively isolable sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_021\", \"rxn_024\", \"rxn_025\", \"rxn_026\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_025\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_029\", \"mol_030\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Thioester-acetoacetate route with pre-reduction ester hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Rewetting hydrolyzes the oxime ester before reduction. Productive ketone reduction would have to trap the free beta-ketoacid skeleton faster than decarboxylation, followed by oxime reduction. This branch has an additional unresolved survival bottleneck and is not asserted to carry a recoverable target flux.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_021\", \"rxn_027\", \"rxn_028\", \"rxn_029\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_025\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_031\", \"mol_032\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_018\",\n    \"mol_027\",\n    \"mol_030\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_026\", \"rxn_029\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_027\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_021\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_022\", \"rxn_024\", \"rxn_027\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_030\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_023\", \"rxn_025\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_026\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Threonine", "overall_strategy": "This is a connected, explicitly conditional synthesis-hypothesis network, not a claim that ten complete prebiotic routes have been experimentally demonstrated. Ten graph-distinct pathways are enumerated across three carbon-skeleton strategies: acetaldehyde addition to glycine or its precursors; unsaturated Strecker chemistry followed by alkene hydration; and nitrosation/reduction of an acetoacetate precursor. Some pathways differ in precursor source or transformation order and are not independent experimental validations. No route requires lactonitrile-to-lactaldehyde photoreduction, and no hydantoin deprotection is assumed. Nevertheless, every pathway contains an unresolved geochemical or substrate-specific bottleneck, identified in the reaction reasoning. Numerical conditions for speculative steps are proposed screening windows, not reported successful preparations or established reaction times. Starting materials are small molecular feedstocks whose local availability must be assessed separately. Reactions require distinct compartments rather than an interchangeable 'surface' environment: aldehyde/ammonia pools, strongly acidic pools, sulfide-containing carbonylation zones, and sulfide-poor hydrogenation interfaces must not be conflated. All chemistry excludes molecular oxygen. Surface reactions occur in shaded pores or beneath sediment skins despite strong external UV. Formulae use neutral-component bookkeeping; actual protonation depends on pH. The target node specifies connectivity without stereochemistry: formation of that connectivity does not establish the threonine/allo-threonine ratio, recoverable threonine yield, or L-enrichment. Hub annotations emphasize organic branching and convergence rather than ubiquitous water and hydrogen.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "ammonia", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "r