ing": "SPECULATIVE. This is a chemically different transformation order with a distinct intermediate, not merely a different environmental label. It may avoid carboxyl-assisted lactonization, but protonated amino-group effects and competing hydrolysis remain. No superiority over hydration after amide hydrolysis is established."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Candidate alkene hydration at the aminonitrile stage", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium from strongly concentrated volcanic chloride acidity"], "conditions": {"temperature": "325-355 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Unvalidated screening window: approximately 0.3-2 M acid with short initial exposures and kinetic sampling. Immediate competing nitrile hydration and retro-Strecker chemistry are expected possibilities."}, "mechanism": "Desired Markovnikov alkene hydration introduces the beta-hydroxyl group while retaining the nitrile.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE. The net transformation is balanced and constitutionally appropriate, but acid-sensitive aminonitrile survival is a particularly serious limitation. This route is retained as a testable reaction-order branch, not evidence that early hydration improves yield or bypasses the general hydration bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Metal-sulfide-associated carbonylation to methyl thioacetate", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_022"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Freshly precipitated FeS/NiS phases"], "conditions": {"temperature": "365-390 K", "pressure": "Proposed 10-100 bar total with a CO-bearing fluid or gas phase", "catalyst": "FeS/NiS-associated nickel and iron sites", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 5.5-7.5, anoxic CO and methanethiol supply, freshly exposed sulfide surfaces. Product export must compete with thioester hydrolysis; environmental CO and thiol concentrations are unresolved."}, "mechanism": "Metal-associated carbonylation and sulfur-acyl transfer produce the acetyl thioester. Neutral net bookkeeping is two methanethiol molecules plus carbon monoxide yielding methyl thioacetate and hydrogen sulfide.", "reasoning": "Activated acetate formation from CO and methanethiol in Fe/Ni-sulfide systems has experimental precedent (Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, 1997). That study does not validate the entire proposed pressure/concentration range or downstream thioester condensation. Fe/Ni sulfides are included for their specific carbonylation chemistry, not as generic prebiotic catalysts."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Candidate thioester Claisen condensation", "inputs": ["mol_023"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ligand-bound Fe2+ as a proposed enolate-stabilizing Lewis acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "315-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hypothetical Fe2+-assisted thioester condensation", "aqueous": true, "other": "Unvalidated screening window: concentrated, partially drying film, pH 9-11. Requires cooling and concentration of exported thioester without prior hydrolysis; no quantitative transport efficiency is assumed."}, "mechanism": "A proposed thioester enolate attacks a second thioester, followed by methanethiolate elimination, producing the beta-ketothioester.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE and the principal carbon-skeleton bottleneck of the acetoacetate family. Thioester Claisen condensation is established in activated synthetic and enzymatic settings, but useful nonenzymatic condensation of methyl thioacetate under these mild aqueous conditions is not demonstrated here. Weak alpha-carbon activation, Fe precipitation, and hydrolysis may prevent accumulation entirely. The mineral-mediated acetyl-thioester precedent must not be extrapolated into evidence for this step."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Beta-ketothioester methanolysis", "inputs": ["mol_024", "mol_