 here. Over-reduction, oxime hydrolysis, and adsorption losses remain possible. Product stereochemistry is not controlled by the proposed achiral catalyst."}, {"id": "rxn_024", "name": "Candidate oxime-first reduction of the beta-ketoester", "inputs": ["mol_027", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_029", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Exposed native Ni-Fe alloy surfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-375 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 5-50 bar", "catalyst": "Native Ni-Fe alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Unvalidated screening window: pH 5.5-7.5, short residence followed by continued hydrogenation. Avoid prolonged storage of the alpha-amino beta-ketoester."}, "mechanism": "Oxime hydrogenation and N-O cleavage introduce the amino group before the ketone is reduced.", "reasoning": "The reaction class is established, but selective accumulation of this amino-ketoester on a native alloy is speculative. Competing ketone reduction and decomposition may prevent isolation. This is a distinct mechanistic order within the same hydrogenation manifold, not an independent source of confidence in the upstream carbon skeleton."}, {"id": "rxn_025", "name": "Ketone reduction after oxime reduction", "inputs": ["mol_029", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_030"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Exposed native Ni-Fe alloy surfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-375 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 5-50 bar", "catalyst": "Native Ni-Fe alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 5.5-7.5; hydrogenation must compete successfully with amino-ketoester condensation, ester hydrolysis, and subsequent beta-ketoacid decarboxylation."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogenation of the remaining ketone forms the beta-hydroxyl group of methyl threoninate.", "reasoning": "Ketone reduction gives the correct amino-alcohol connectivity. A sufficiently rapid mineral-catalyzed reaction before substrate degradation is an unresolved operational requirement. The ester protects against immediate beta-ketoacid decarboxylation only while it remains intact."}, {"id": "rxn_026", "name": "Methyl threoninate hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_030", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium from volcanic chloride acidity"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-2, water-rich receiving pool after cooling from the hydrogenation zone; hours-to-days screening followed by dilution or cooling."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed ester hydrolysis releases the amino acid and methanol.", "reasoning": "Ester hydrolysis is a conventional and generally less demanding deprotection than hydantoin cleavage. It avoids the previous hydantoin bottleneck, but substrate-specific recovery and the necessary acid compartment still require verification. The reaction does not establish which stereoisomers were formed upstream."}, {"id": "rxn_027", "name": "Alternative ester hydrolysis before reduction", "inputs": ["mol_027", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_031", "mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium from volcanic chloride acidity"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Unvalidated pH 1-2 screening window, followed by prompt transfer and pH adjustment into a hydrogenating interface. Measure decarboxylation throughout hydrolysis, not only after transfer."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis removes the methyl ester while retaining the oxime and ketone in the desired channel.", "reasoning": "Ester hydrolysis is established, and this branch represents a chemically possible consequence of rewetting rather than an additional activation step. However, the resulting oxime-containing beta-keto acid may decarboxylate before reduction. A recoverable flux through this branch is u