echanism under the proposed aqueous or concentrated-pore conditions.\",\n        \"Use structural analysis to distinguish the desired beta-hydroxy aminonitrile from imines, oligomers, and constitutional isomers.\",\n        \"Measure sequential nitrile and amide hydrolysis yields together with carbon-skeleton retention.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.7,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.44,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.75,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.31\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.49,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.61,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.44,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.24,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.38,\n      \"feedback\": \"Cross-aldol formation of 3-hydroxypropanal and dehydration to acrolein are plausible, although competing condensation, hydration, and polymerization need control. Acrolein Strecker chemistry is not assured because conjugate addition and oligomerization can compete with the desired alpha-aminonitrile formation. The final Markovnikov hydration has the correct net regiochemistry for threonine connectivity, but useful hydration of vinylglycine under compatible acidic conditions is not demonstrated. Protonation of the amino group, alkene isomerization, and substrate degradation require assessment.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Establish the product distribution of acrolein, ammonia, and cyanide under the actual proposed conditions.\",\n        \"Test vinylglycine hydration independently and quantify regioisomers, isomerization products, decomposition, and recovered substrate.\",\n        \"Specify acrolein handling and transfer conditions that limit volatilization and polymerization.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_sco