onal route to the target.', 'The proposed aminonitrile has the appropriate carbon skeleton, and its hydration followed by amide hydrolysis is chemically reasonable. The unresolved step is rxn_007: aminoacetonitrile must become sufficiently carbon-nucleophilic to add to acetaldehyde under conditions that preserve the substrate and favor C-C rather than N-condensation chemistry. Neither the activation mechanism nor its environmental implementation is established. Subsequent hydrolysis also needs to preserve the beta-hydroxy skeleton.', 'Cross-aldol formation of 3-hydroxypropanal and dehydration to acrolein are plausible, although competing condensation, hydration, and polymerization need control. Acrolein Strecker chemistry is not assured because conjugate addition and oligomerization can compete with the desired alpha-aminonitrile formation. The final Markovnikov hydration has the correct net regiochemistry for threonine connectivity, but useful hydration of vinylglycine under compatible acidic conditions is not demonstrated. Protonation of the amino group, alkene isomerization, and substrate degradation require assessment.', 'This pathway inherits the uncertain acrolein Strecker selectivity. Moving alkene hydration to vinylglycinamide does not independently establish that hydration will occur at useful rates or with the required regiochemistry. Conditions sufficiently acidic for alkene hydration may also hydrolyze the amide or promote other alkene chemistry. Such concurrent reactions need not make the entire network impossible, but they leave this specific stage-ordered route unsubstantiated. Final threoninamide hydrolysis is otherwise credible.', 'In addition to uncertain acrolein Strecker chemistry, rxn_017 requires useful alkene hydration at the aminonitrile stage. Acidic aqueous conditions can also hydrate or hydrolyze the nitrile and affect aminonitrile stability, so the stipulated transformation order is not established. Competing nitrile hydration could redir