n, and subsequent formamide hydrolysis require measurement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Formamide hydrolysis supplying ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ion supplied by an acidic, chloride-bearing fluid\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393\u2013423 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20\u2013100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded fluid; cooled-sample pH 0.5\u20132.0. Screen 10\u2013200 mM formamide. Ammonia is predominantly retained as ammonium under these conditions. Before Strecker chemistry, cool to 300\u2013323 K and mix with alkaline water; verify the final ammonia concentration and pH rather than assuming transfer preserves them.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl; cleavage of the carbon\u2013nitrogen bond produces formic acid and ammonia, predominantly ammonium in the acidic compartment.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formamide hydrolysis is experimentally characterized in origin-of-life stability studies (Miyakawa, Cleaves and Miller, 2002). It is a credible nitrogen-release reaction, but useful ammonia accumulation depends on conversion, fluid residence time, and subsequent dilution. Formamide may be externally supplied in P2 and P7 or generated by rxn_001 in P3 and P8. No target-specific catalytic mineral is needed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanamide hydration to urea\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ion supplied by volcanogenic acidity\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323\u2013353 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded volcanic pool or pore water; pH 1\u20132. Screen 1\u201350 mM cyanamide. Avoid strongly concentrating cyanamide before conversion because dimerization and other condensation reactions compete. Transfer urea-containing water to the warmer compartment used in rxn_004 only after analytical confirmation of urea formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-assisted hydration of the nitrile group of cyanamide yields urea.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanamide-to-urea hydration is established reaction-class chemistry and provides a chemically meaningful alternative nitrogen inventory. No study supplied with the request establishes its conversion or selectivity in this particular environmental window, so its geochemical implementation remains conditional. The acidic window is proposed to favor hydration over the condensation chemistry that can consume cyanamide. This reaction is included only for pathways whose supplied nitrogen feedstock is cyanamide.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Net thermal hydrolysis of urea\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393\u2013423 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20\u2013100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic liquid; cooled-sample pH 4\u20136, with sufficient acidic buffering to prevent released ammonia from making the compartment strongly alkaline. Screen 10\u2013200 mM urea. Complete cyanate destruction before introducing amino-containing Strecker products; residual cyanate can carbamylate them. Cool and adjust pH before transfer to rxn_005.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thermal decomposition through the ammonium\u2013cyanate manifold, followed by cyanate hydrolysis, gives the net products ammonia and carbon dioxide. Two ammonia molecules are produced per urea molecule; coefficients are omitted from the graph.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thermal urea decomposition in aqueous media has direct experimental precedent (Shaw and Bordeaux, 1955). The node represents a net reaction, not an elementary concerted hydrolysis. Urea is more persistent than a freely hydrolyzing activated nitrogen donor, so its decomposition rate can limit these pathways. The cited precedent does not establish the necessary geological residence time, and residual cyanate is an important incompatibility with downstream amino compounds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible lactaldehyde imine formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, opaque mineral pore or covered pool margin; pH 8.3\u20139.2. Proposed screening concentrations: 1\u201320 mM lactaldehyde and 50\u2013500 mM total ammonia/ammonium. Use a small, non-flushed headspace, preferably below 0.1 times the liquid volume, and measure volatile losses. Conduct rxn_006 concurrently; do not assume an isolable or highly accumulated imine.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the aldehyde to form a carbinolamine, which reversibly loses water to the imine. Cyanide trapping in rxn_006 can draw material through a low steady-state imine population.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aldehyde imination followed by cyanide capture is the established Strecker reaction framework (Strecker, 1850). This is reaction-class support, not a reported lactaldehyde-specific yield under the proposed conditions. Lactaldehyde hydration, oligomerization, and competing condensation must be quantified. Supplying lactaldehyde avoids claiming an unsupported selective precursor reduction, but does not establish that lactaldehyde was geochemically available.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture forming the threonine carbon skeleton\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same anoxic, shaded compartment as rxn_005; pH 8.3\u20139.2. Screen 5\u2013100 mM total HCN/cyanide, supplied separately from any fraction hydrolyzed in rxn_001. Measure aminonitrile formation, residual aldehyde, cyanohydrin formation, and total cyanide loss. No mineral catalyst or UV irradiation is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, giving CH3\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH(NH2)\u2013CN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the carbon\u2013carbon bond-forming step of the classical Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). The connectivity directly places the amino and hydroxy substituents required for threonine after nitrile hydrolysis. Competing aldehyde cyanohydrin formation is an equilibrium competitor, not counted here as an independent validated synthesis route. Without a stereocontrolling agent, formation of both threonine-family diastereomers must be expected, with unestablished proportions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ion\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373\u2013413 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20\u2013100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Separate anoxic, UV-shielded, acidic chloride-bearing compartment; cooled-sample pH 0.5\u20131.5. Screen 1\u201320 mM aminonitrile and residence times of 0.5\u201372 hours as experiments, not assumed geological conversion times. Avoid continuous gas stripping and strongly rock-buffered fluids that would neutralize the required acidity. Follow amide accumulation and carbon-skeleton loss analytically.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation, water addition, and tautomerization produce the primary carboxamide. The amino group is predominantly protonated during the reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitrile hydrolysis is part of the established Strecker framework (Strecker, 1850), but substrate-specific rates are needed for this beta-hydroxy aminonitrile. The amide is represented because hydration and subsequent amide cleavage need not have the same rate. Acidic hydrolysis is not assumed to be compatible with the alkaline Strecker compartment; it occurs only after a specified environmental transfer.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Threoninamide hydrolysis to the amino-acid product pool\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ion\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373\u2013413 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20\u2013100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same acidic compartment as rxn_007, or a downstream compartment with cooled-sample pH 0.5\u20131.5. Screen 0.5\u201372 hours and stop heating when recoverable amino-acid yield reaches its maximum. Cool recovered material below 323 K and adjust toward pH 5\u20137 for storage. Quantify threonine, allo-threonine, remaining amide, and degradation products separately.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the amide carbonyl and carbon\u2013nitrogen bond cleavage produce the amino acid and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Primary-amide hydrolysis is established chemistry, but useful net production requires hydrolysis to outpace destruction of the beta-hydroxy amino acid. Analytical amino-acid hydrolysis literature documents that threonine recovery is sensitive to hot-acid exposure (Fountoulakis and Lahm, 1998). That literature warns against assuming quantitative survival; it does not validate the proposed dilute-acid window. The temperature\u2013residence-time tradeoff is a principal experimental bottleneck of the direct branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional sulfide interception of the aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"303\u2013333 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded, semiclosed sulfide-bearing pore water; pH 8\u20139.5. Screen 1\u201320 mM aminonitrile and 5\u2013100 mM total dissolved sulfide. Establish the actual dissolved sulfide concentration after equilibration with Fe-bearing minerals; bulk sulfide addition does not establish HS\u2212 availability. Compare product formation against an otherwise identical sulfide-free control.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfers and tautomerization yield the primary thioamide. Hydrogen sulfide is a stoichiometric reactant, not a mineral catalyst.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile-to-thioamide conversion by sulfide has reaction-class precedent, but no substrate-specific study or rate measurement for this aminonitrile under the stated prebiotic conditions is supplied here. Consequently this step is explicitly hypothetical in this network. It is included because sulfide-rich settings can chemically intercept nitriles, not because it has been shown to improve threonine yield. If the thioamide is only a persistent sink, P6\u2013P10 are not productive pathways.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional exhaustive thioamide hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium ion\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393\u2013413 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20\u2013100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Separate anoxic, UV-shielded acidic compartment; cooled-sample pH 0.5\u20131.5. Screen 0.5\u201372 hours with product mass balance, including dissolved and headspace sulfur. Stop and cool when recoverable amino-acid yield is maximal. Neither molecular oxygen nor oxidative desulfurization is used. Successful transfer from the alkaline sulfide compartment and productive hydrolysis remain experimental requirements.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Successive hydrolytic substitutions at the thioamide carbon give the carboxylic acid, ammonia, and hydrogen sulfide. This is a net reaction consuming two water molecules per thioamide; it does not prescribe a selectively isolated oxygen-amide intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolytic thioamide cleavage is chemically established at the reaction-class level, but productive cleavage of this beta-hydroxy amino thioamide before amino-acid degradation is not established by the evidence available here. This target-producing step is therefore explicitly speculative and is the main additional bottleneck of P6\u2013P10. Threonine's sensitivity to prolonged hot-acid treatment makes a measured yield-versus-time curve essential (Fountoulakis and Lahm, 1998). Atom conservation alone cannot validate this branch.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied ammonia with direct Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied lactaldehyde, ammonia, and HCN enter the Strecker sequence, followed by nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis. This is the shortest reaction-class-supported route in the network, conditional on lactaldehyde availability and recoverable product yield.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Formamide-derived ammonia with direct Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied formamide is hydrolyzed to release ammonia. After cooling and pH adjustment, that ammonia reacts with supplied lactaldehyde and a separate HCN feed. The carbon-skeleton construction and final hydrolysis are shared with P1.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Partially hydrolyzed HCN nitrogen supply with direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"One fraction of an HCN inventory is hydrolyzed through formamide to supply ammonia. A separate, unhydrolyzed fraction supplies the nitrile carbon in the Strecker reaction with lactaldehyde. This pathway requires stream separation and does not reuse hydrolyzed cyanide as a carbon donor.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Urea-derived ammonia with direct Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied urea releases ammonia in a warm aqueous compartment. Residual cyanate must be destroyed before the cooled nitrogen-containing stream meets lactaldehyde and HCN. The direct nitrile-to-amide-to-amino-acid sequence then follows.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanamide-to-urea nitrogen supply with direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied cyanamide is hydrated to urea, which subsequently supplies ammonia by thermal decomposition and hydrolysis. After cooling and conditioning, lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry and direct hydrolysis produce the target connectivity. Cyanamide condensation and slow urea decomposition are additional upstream limitations.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied ammonia with sulfide-intercepted Strecker chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied ammonia, lactaldehyde, and HCN form the aminonitrile, which is intercepted by sulfide before exhaustive hydrolysis. The sulfur-containing intermediate makes this graph-distinct from P1, but both its formation and productive hydrolysis require substrate-specific validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Formamide-derived ammonia with sulfide interception\",\n      \"description\": \"Formamide hydrolysis supplies ammonia for lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry. Sulfide interception then leads to the hypothetical thioamide hydrolysis branch. This combines a supported nitrogen-release reaction with an unvalidated sulfur-mediated finish.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Partially hydrolyzed HCN nitrogen supply with sulfide interception\",\n      \"description\": \"Separate HCN fractions supply ammonia through hydrolysis and nitrile carbon through Strecker addition. The resulting aminonitrile enters the conditional sulfide/thioamide branch. Cyanide retention, dissolved sulfide availability, and thioamide hydrolysis must all be demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Urea-derived ammonia with sulfide interception\",\n      \"description\": \"Urea-derived ammonia enters the shared Strecker sequence after cyanate removal. Dissolved sulfide then intercepts the aminonitrile. This pathway remains conditional on the thioamide being a productive intermediate rather than a sulfur-containing sink.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanamide-to-urea nitrogen supply with sulfide interception\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanamide hydration and urea decomposition provide ammonia, followed by lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry, sulfide interception, and hypothetical exhaustive thioamide hydrolysis. This is the longest conditional route and is not independent evidence for the shared carbon-skeleton chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_015\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Threonine", "overall_strategy": "This is a conditional network containing ten graph-distinct pathways, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses. Five chemically different ammonia-supply options feed a shared lactaldehyde Strecker sequence, followed either by ordinary nitrile hydrolysis or by a sulfide-intercepted thioamide branch. Lactaldehyde is explicitly supplied as a simple C3 organic feedstock: its geochemical availability is an unresolved boundary condition, rather than being concealed behind an unvalidated selective photoreduction. No glycine aldol reaction, selective lactonitrile reduction, hydantoin deprotection, or unspecified mineral reduction is assumed. The sulfur branch is less well supported and is explicitly identified as hypothetical for this substrate. Numerical conditions are proposed experimental screening windows, not published substrate-specific optima or established geological residence times. Transfers require cooling ammonia-producing streams to 300\u2013323 K, adjustment to the stated Strecker pH, and verification that dilution and volatilization have not lowered reagent concentrations below the specified windows. Organic products subsequently enter separate, UV-shielded acidic hydrothermal compartments for hydrolysis. All compartments are anoxic. Reduced iron can be abundant regionally, but sulfide interception requires measurable dissolved sulfide after FeS precipitation. Molecular formulas use neutral parent-species bookkeeping; actual protonation follows local pH. The target node records constitutional connectivity without stereochemical specification: threonine and allo-threonine may both occur, and neither their proportions nor L-threonine enrichment is established. Terminal coproducts use the schema's intermediate role because it has no byproduct category.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C3H6O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanal", "common_name": "Lactaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Ammonia", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CH3NO", "iupac_name": "Formamide", "common_name": "Formamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH3NO/c2-1-3/h1H,(H2,2,3)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CH2O2", "iupac_name": "Methanoic acid", "common_name": "Formic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O2/c2-1-3/h1H,(H,2,3)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CH2N2", "iupac_name": "Cyanamide", "common_name": "Cyanamide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "CH4N2O", "iupac_name": "Urea", "common_name": "Urea", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4N2O/c2-1(3)4/h(H4,2,3,4)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H7NO", "iupac_name": "1-iminopropan-2-ol", "common_name": "Lactaldehyde imine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "Threonine-family aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide", "common_name": "Threoninamide-family stereoisomer pool", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C4H10N2OS", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanethioamide", "common_name": "Threoninethioamide-family stereoisomer pool", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid", "common_name": "Threonine/allo-threonine stereoisomer pool", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H9NO3/c1-2(6)3(5)4(7)8/h2-3,6H,5H2,1H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Hydrogen cyanide hydration to formamide", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ion supplied by an acidic, chloride-bearing fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373\u2013413 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "20\u2013100 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV-shielded, anoxic, weakly rock-buffered acidic pocket; pH 0.5\u20132.0, referenced to a cooled sample. Screen 1\u201350 mM total HCN in a closed liquid-dominated compartment without continuous gas stripping. For pathways retaining HCN as the Strecker carbon donor, hydrolyze only a separate fraction of the HCN inventory. Geological residence time must be determined from measured conversion and competing loss."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted water addition to the nitrile, followed by proton transfers and tautomerization, gives formamide.", "reasoning": "HCN hydrolysis and the temperature-dependent persistence of HCN and formamide have experimental precedent (Miyakawa, Cleaves and Miller, 2002). That study supports the reaction family, not the exact proposed operating window. This branch deliberately sacrifices some cyanide to supply ammonia downstream; it cannot simultaneously assume quantitative preservation of the same cyanide inventory for Strecker chemistry. Volatilization, oligomerization, and subsequent formamide hydrolysis require measurement."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Formamide hydrolysis supplying ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ion supplied by an acidic, chloride-bearing fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393\u2013423 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "20\u2013100 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded fluid; cooled-sample pH 0.5\u20132.0. Screen 10\u2013200 mM formamide. Ammonia is predominantly retained as ammonium under these conditions. Before Strecker chemistry, cool to 300\u2013323 K and mix with alkaline water; verify the final ammonia concentration and pH rather than assuming transfer preserves them."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl; cleavage of the carbon\u2013nitrogen bond produces formic acid and ammonia, predominantly ammonium in the acidic compartment.", "reasoning": "Formamide hydrolysis is experimentally characterized in origin-of-life stability studies (Miyakawa, Cleaves and Miller, 2002). It is a credible nitrogen-release reaction, but useful ammonia accumulation depends on conversion, fluid residence time, and subsequent dilution. Formamide may be externally supplied in P2 and P7 or generated by rxn_001 in P3 and P8. No target-specific catalytic mineral is needed."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cyanamide hydration to urea", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ion supplied by volcanogenic acidity"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323\u2013353 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded volcanic pool or pore water; pH 1\u20132. Screen 1\u201350 mM cyanamide. Avoid strongly concentrating cyanamide before conversion because dimerization and other condensation reactions compete. Transfer urea-containing water to the warmer compartment used in rxn_004 only after analytical confirmation of urea formation."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted hydration of the nitrile group of cyanamide yields urea.", "reasoning": "Cyanamide-to-urea hydration is established reaction-class chemistry and provides a chemically meaningful alternative nitrogen inventory. No study supplied with the request establishes its conversion or selectivity in this particular environmental window, so its geochemical implementation remains conditional. The acidic window is proposed to favor hydration over the condensation chemistry that can consume cyanamide. This reaction is included only for pathways whose supplied nitrogen feedstock is cyanamide."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Net thermal hydrolysis of urea", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_004", "mol_009"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "393\u2013423 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "20\u2013100 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic liquid; cooled-sample pH 4\u20136, with sufficient acidic buffering to prevent released ammonia from making the compartment strongly alkaline. Screen 10\u2013200 mM urea. Complete cyanate destruction before introducing amino-containing Strecker products; residual cyanate can carbamylate them. Cool and adjust pH before transfer to rxn_005."}, "mechanism": "Thermal decomposition through the ammonium\u2013cyanate manifold, followed by cyanate hydrolysis, gives the net products ammonia and carbon dioxide. Two ammonia molecules are produced per urea molecule; coefficients are omitted from the graph.", "reasoning": "Thermal urea decomposition in aqueous media has direct experimental precedent (Shaw and Bordeaux, 1955). The node represents a net reaction, not an elementary concerted hydrolysis. Urea is more persistent than a freely hydrolyzing activated nitrogen donor, so its decomposition rate can limit these pathways. The cited precedent does not establish the necessary geological residence time, and residual cyanate is an important incompatibility with downstream amino compounds."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Reversible lactaldehyde imine formation", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, opaque mineral pore or covered pool margin; pH 8.3\u20139.2. Proposed screening concentrations: 1\u201320 mM lactaldehyde and 50\u2013500 mM total ammonia/ammonium. Use a small, non-flushed headspace, preferably below 0.1 times the liquid volume, and measure volatile losses. Conduct rxn_006 concurrently; do not assume an isolable or highly accumulated imine."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde to form a carbinolamine, which reversibly loses water to the imine. Cyanide trapping in rxn_006 can draw material through a low steady-state imine population.", "reasoning": "Aldehyde imination followed by cyanide capture is the established Strecker reaction framework (Strecker, 1850). This is reaction-class support, not a reported lactaldehyde-specific yield under the proposed conditions. Lactaldehyde hydration, oligomerization, and competing condensation must be quantified. Supplying lactaldehyde avoids claiming an unsupported selective precursor reduction, but does not establish that lactaldehyde was geochemically available."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Cyanide capture forming the threonine carbon skeleton", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Same anoxic, shaded compartment as rxn_005; pH 8.3\u20139.2. Screen 5\u2013100 mM total HCN/cyanide, supplied separately from any fraction hydrolyzed in rxn_001. Measure aminonitrile formation, residual aldehyde, cyanohydrin formation, and total cyanide loss. No mineral catalyst or UV irradiation is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, giving CH3\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH(NH2)\u2013CN.", "reasoning": "This is the carbon\u2013carbon bond-forming step of the classical Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). The connectivity directly places the amino and hydroxy substituents required for threonine after nitrile hydrolysis. Competing aldehyde cyanohydrin formation is an equilibrium competitor, not counted here as an independent validated synthesis route. Without a stereocontrolling agent, formation of both threonine-family diastereomers must be expected, with unestablished proportions."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Direct aminonitrile hydration to threoninamide", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373\u2013413 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "20\u2013100 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Separate anoxic, UV-shielded, acidic chloride-bearing compartment; cooled-sample pH 0.5\u20131.5. Screen 1\u201320 mM aminonitrile and residence times of 0.5\u201372 hours as experiments, not assumed geological conversion times. Avoid continuous gas stripping and strongly rock-buffered fluids that would neutralize the required acidity. Follow amide accumulation and carbon-skeleton loss analytically."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation, water addition, and tautomerization produce the primary carboxamide. The amino group is predominantly protonated during the reaction.", "reasoning": "Aminonitrile hydrolysis is part of the established Strecker framework (Strecker, 1850), but substrate-specific rates are needed for this beta-hydroxy aminonitrile. The amide is represented because hydration and subsequent amide cleavage need not have the same rate. Acidic hydrolysis is not assumed to be compatible with the alkaline Strecker compartment; it occurs only after a specified environmental transfer."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Threoninamide hydrolysis to the amino-acid product pool", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373\u2013413 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "20\u2013100 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same acidic compartment as rxn_007, or a downstream compartment with cooled-sample pH 0.5\u20131.5. Screen 0.5\u201372 hours and stop heating when recoverable amino-acid yield reaches its maximum. Cool recovered material below 323 K and adjust toward pH 5\u20137 for storage. Quantify threonine, allo-threonine, remaining amide, and degradation products separately."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the amide carbonyl and carbon\u2013nitrogen bond cleavage produce the amino acid and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Primary-amide hydrolysis is established chemistry, but useful net production requires hydrolysis to outpace destruction of the beta-hydroxy amino acid. Analytical amino-acid hydrolysis literature documents that threonine recovery is sensitive to hot-acid exposure (Fountoulakis and Lahm, 1998). That literature warns against assuming quantitative survival; it does not validate the proposed dilute-acid window. The temperature\u2013residence-time tradeoff is a principal experimental bottleneck of the direct branch."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Conditional sulfide interception of the aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "303\u2013333 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded, semiclosed sulfide-bearing pore water; pH 8\u20139.5. Screen 1\u201320 mM aminonitrile and 5\u2013100 mM total dissolved sulfide. Establish the actual dissolved sulfide concentration after equilibration with Fe-bearing minerals; bulk sulfide addition does not establish HS\u2212 availability. Compare product formation against an otherwise identical sulfide-free control."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfers and tautomerization yield the primary thioamide. Hydrogen sulfide is a stoichiometric reactant, not a mineral catalyst.", "reasoning": "Nitrile-to-thioamide conversion by sulfide has reaction-class precedent, but no substrate-specific study or rate measurement for this aminonitrile under the stated prebiotic conditions is supplied here. Consequently this step is explicitly hypothetical in this network. It is included because sulfide-rich settings can chemically intercept nitriles, not because it has been shown to improve threonine yield. If the thioamide is only a persistent sink, P6\u2013P10 are not productive pathways."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Conditional exhaustive thioamide hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004", "mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium ion"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393\u2013413 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "20\u2013100 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Separate anoxic, UV-shielded acidic compartment; cooled-sample pH 0.5\u20131.5. Screen 0.5\u201372 hours with product mass balance, including dissolved and headspace sulfur. Stop and cool when recoverable amino-acid yield is maximal. Neither molecular oxygen nor oxidative desulfurization is used. Successful transfer from the alkaline sulfide compartment and productive hydrolysis remain experimental requirements."}, "mechanism": "Successive hydrolytic substitutions at the thioamide carbon give the carboxylic acid, ammonia, and hydrogen sulfide. This is a net reaction consuming two water molecules per thioamide; it does not prescribe a selectively isolated oxygen-amide intermediate.", "reasoning": "Hydrolytic thioamide cleavage is chemically established at the reaction-class level, but productive cleavage of this beta-hydroxy amino thioamide before amino-acid degradation is not established by the evidence available here. This target-producing step is therefore explicitly speculative and is the main additional bottleneck of P6\u2013P10. Threonine's sensitivity to prolonged hot-acid treatment makes a measured yield-versus-time curve essential (Fountoulakis and Lahm, 1998). Atom conservation alone cannot validate this branch."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Supplied ammonia with direct Strecker hydrolysis", "description": "Externally supplied lactaldehyde, ammonia, and HCN enter the Strecker sequence, followed by nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis. This is the shortest reaction-class-supported route in the network, conditional on lactaldehyde availability and recoverable product yield.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Formamide-derived ammonia with direct Strecker hydrolysis", "description": "Externally supplied formamide is hydrolyzed to release ammonia. After cooling and pH adjustment, that ammonia reacts with supplied lactaldehyde and a separate HCN feed. The carbon-skeleton construction and final hydrolysis are shared with P1.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Partially hydrolyzed HCN nitrogen supply with direct hydrolysis", "description": "One fraction of an HCN inventory is hydrolyzed through formamide to supply ammonia. A separate, unhydrolyzed fraction supplies the nitrile carbon in the Strecker reaction with lactaldehyde. This pathway requires stream separation and does not reuse hydrolyzed cyanide as a carbon donor.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Urea-derived ammonia with direct Strecker hydrolysis", "description": "Externally supplied urea releases ammonia in a warm aqueous compartment. Residual cyanate must be destroyed before the cooled nitrogen-containing stream meets lactaldehyde and HCN. The direct nitrile-to-amide-to-amino-acid sequence then follows.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_004", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Cyanamide-to-urea nitrogen supply with direct hydrolysis", "description": "Supplied cyanamide is hydrated to urea, which subsequently supplies ammonia by thermal decomposition and hydrolysis. After cooling and conditioning, lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry and direct hydrolysis produce the target connectivity. Cyanamide condensation and slow urea decomposition are additional upstream limitations.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_004", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Supplied ammonia with sulfide-intercepted Strecker chemistry", "description": "Supplied ammonia, lactaldehyde, and HCN form the aminonitrile, which is intercepted by sulfide before exhaustive hydrolysis. The sulfur-containing intermediate makes this graph-distinct from P1, but both its formation and productive hydrolysis require substrate-specific validation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Formamide-derived ammonia with sulfide interception", "description": "Formamide hydrolysis supplies ammonia for lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry. Sulfide interception then leads to the hypothetical thioamide hydrolysis branch. This combines a supported nitrogen-release reaction with an unvalidated sulfur-mediated finish.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_011", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Partially hydrolyzed HCN nitrogen supply with sulfide interception", "description": "Separate HCN fractions supply ammonia through hydrolysis and nitrile carbon through Strecker addition. The resulting aminonitrile enters the conditional sulfide/thioamide branch. Cyanide retention, dissolved sulfide availability, and thioamide hydrolysis must all be demonstrated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_011", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Urea-derived ammonia with sulfide interception", "description": "Urea-derived ammonia enters the shared Strecker sequence after